<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walata, or Oualata, is an oasis town in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> that was an important terminus of trans-Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. The nearby escarpment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhar-walata">Dhar Walata</a> is associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tichitt-culture">Tichitt culture</a> from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> emerged. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> profile notes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> forces seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and Walata in 1433, a sign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s weakening hold on the north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walata.jpg" alt="Walata" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: c.hug · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oualata_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
